  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Current model guidance continues to show\na series of waves riding the flow of a parent trough over the Pac\nNW into the Great Basin beginning Friday and lasting through this\nweekend. The first wave is a more subtle weaker wave that will \npass through the region Friday. Current satellite imagery shows an\nincrease in moisture and cloud cover across the area. This trend \nwill continue and precipitation associated with the wave is \nexpected to begin by early Friday AM. The first wave will be\nthrough the region by Saturday AM and will feature light valley\nrain and mountain snow as snow levels remain above 8500 feet\nacross the region. Friday's rainfall totals in the valleys are the\nlightest of the weekend ranging 0.05-0.15 inches. Saturday trends\nin precipitation type are expected to continue but QPF jumps a bit\nespecially over north central NV where amounts will range 0.1-0.3\ninches of rainfall. \n\nThe main trough associated with the LPC to the north-northwest and\nstronger of the two waves will impact the region Saturday PM\nthrough Sunday. Highest precipitation rates across northern and\ncentral NV are expected Saturday evening into Sunday. The best\nchances for snowfall in valleys will continue Sunday PM as snow\nlevels fall to valley floors behind frontal passage on Sunday.\nSnow showers could bring a trace to an inch of snowfall along\nelevated roadways and mountain passes. This is especially true for\nportions of SR-225 between Elko and Owyhee, and along US-50 from\nAustin to GBNP. Sunday and early Monday AM will be the prime\nsnowpack loading time for the regions high elevations. Peaks and\nridges will take on 6 inches to a foot of fresh snowfall as the\nwave passes through the area. WPC 1-5 day QPF totals for the\nweekend will range 0.25 to 1.25 inches of precipitation with\nhigher amounts in eastern NV especially in White Pine County. \n\nMonday afternoon a rapid dry out will occur behind the wave when\nnorthwest flow and a PFR regime will setup over the Great Basin. \nBrief ridging next week over the southwestern CONUS will reign \nthrough mid week ahead of another LPC long range guidance has \nsetting up over the Pacific potentially impacting the area next \nweekend.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate confidence remains in\nmeasurable precipitation Friday through Sunday. Low confidence\ncontinues for snowfall amounts related to lower elevations on\nSunday. Moderate confidence is building towards a rapid dryout\nnext Monday. No major grid edits were needed at this time.",
